{%- comment -%}
  Reference section. Copy this file, its module folder and its SCSS partial as
  the starting point for a new one. Four things it exists to demonstrate:

  1. The <__NS__-example> tag is what mounts the component. No data-section-type,
     no registry, no boot scan — and no JavaScript at all until the tag is on
     the page, which is the point.
  2. Loading priority comes from `section.index`. The first section on a page
     holds the LCP candidate, so `__NS__-image` loads it eagerly; below that it
     lazy-loads. Always pass `section_index: section.index`.
  3. Every image slot names a placeholder. See below.
  4. Motion belongs to <__NS__-reveal>. It arms the hidden state itself, so text
     stays visible when the bundle fails, and it honours reduced motion.
  5. Settings reach the component as one escaped JSON blob, not as a dozen
     data attributes.

  ## Empty image slots

  A merchant adds a section before they have the photography for it. An image
  slot that renders nothing collapses the layout, so the section reads as broken
  when it is merely unfilled — and the merchant cannot see the shape they are
  filling. So: **every `__NS__-image` call passes a `placeholder`.**

      {%- render '__NS__-image',
        image: section.settings.image,
        placeholder: 'image',
        section_index: section.index,
        classes: '__NS__-example__image'
      -%}

  `__NS__-image` draws Shopify's `placeholder_svg_tag` whenever the image is blank,
  and puts `classes` on the placeholder as well as on the real `<img>`. The slot
  therefore holds the same box either way and nothing moves when the image lands
  — so size the image in SCSS through that shared class, never through a wrapper
  that only exists on one branch of an `{% if %}`.

  Pick the placeholder that matches the slot, not whatever is shortest:

  | Slot                        | Name                                |
  |-----------------------------|-------------------------------------|
  | Product media               | `product-1` … `product-6`           |
  | Collection or category tile | `collection-1` … `collection-6`     |
  | Full-bleed or hero media    | `lifestyle-1`, `lifestyle-2`        |
  | Editorial card, logo, generic | `image`                           |

  The placeholder is unconditional — it renders on the storefront, not only in
  the theme editor. An empty slot is the same empty slot either way, and a
  section that looks right in the editor and collapses on the storefront is
  worse than one that is honestly unfilled in both.

  One slot takes no placeholder: **decorative art** — a texture, a line-art
  flourish, anything `aria-hidden`. It is absent by design, and a placeholder
  would invent a focal point the section never had. Leave it gated on the image:

      {%- if section.settings.panel_texture != blank -%}

  ## Mobile art direction

  Every image slot has an `<id>_mobile` companion, and it is paired in two
  places — the schema and the render:

      {
        "type": "image_picker",
        "id": "image_mobile",
        "label": "Mobile image",
        "info": "Optional. Replaces the image below 768px. …",
        "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.image != blank }}"
      }

      {%- render '__NS__-image',
        image: section.settings.image,
        mobile_image: section.settings.image_mobile,
        placeholder: 'image',
      -%}

  `visible_if` keeps the mobile picker out of the sidebar until the desktop
  image exists, because a mobile override with nothing to override is noise in a
  panel a merchant has to read top to bottom. Use `block.settings.` for a block.

  `__NS__-image` turns the pair into a `<picture>` with a `(max-width: 767.98px)`
  source, so the browser fetches one file. Never reach for a second `<img>`
  hidden by CSS — a hidden image still downloads, and the visitor on the slower
  connection pays for both.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign image_alt = ''
  if section.settings.heading != blank
    assign image_alt = section.settings.heading | append: ' image'
  endif
-%}

{%- capture config -%}
{"heading": {{ section.settings.heading | json }}, "buttonLabel": {{ section.settings.button_label | json }}}
{%- endcapture -%}

<__NS__-example data-config="{{ config | escape }}">
  {%- render '__NS__-image',
    image: section.settings.image,
    mobile_image: section.settings.image_mobile,
    alt: image_alt,
    placeholder: 'image',
    sizes: '(min-width: 768px) 50vw, 100vw',
    section_index: section.index,
    classes: '__NS__-example__image'
  -%}

  <__NS__-reveal class="__NS__-example__body">
    {%- render '__NS__-heading',
      text: section.settings.heading,
      tag: 'h2',
      size: 'h1',
      animation: 'rise',
      classes: '__NS__-example__heading'
    -%}

    <button type="button" class="__NS__-example__action" data-ref="action" data-reveal>
      {{- section.settings.button_label -}}
    </button>
  </__NS__-reveal>
</__NS__-example>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "__NS_TITLE__ example",
  "tag": "section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "heading",
      "label": "Heading",
      "default": "Example section"
    },
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "image",
      "label": "Image"
    },
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "image_mobile",
      "label": "Mobile image",
      "info": "Optional. Replaces the image below 768px. One request either way — it becomes a <picture> source, not a second download.",
      "visible_if": "{{ section.settings.image != blank }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "button_label",
      "label": "Button label",
      "default": "Toggle"
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "__NS_TITLE__ example"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
